congressman john lewis's legacy has  inspired a new generation of civil  rights activists including those in the  black lives matter movement  jerika duncan spoke with two co-founders  of the movement patrice cullers  and opal time she also talked with dr  bernice king the daughter of dr martin  luther king jr  they discussed congressman lewis's fight  for equality and how it can be carried  on  what did you all learn from him and how  are you using his message  to continue your efforts in black lives  matter  you know i think i learned that this is  a long-haul fight and struggle that the  work that we do  matters most if we stay inside of it and  if we recognize that nothing changes  overnight  my father defined a revolt as moving  people  but a revolution as changing people and  systems so the question is whether  we will see the true change in  manifestation  that this movement now becomes  revolutionary who do you see as that  next group of people to leave this  movement we do need to ensure that there  are people  uh that are spokespersons even if it's  20  and that the messaging is the same you  don't have to literally  say the exact same words but the the  narrative and the focus  and the target that those people who  represent  this generation are sending that same  message  so much of what we get to do in our day  is  because of the tremendous work that our  ancestors and freedom fighters  before us did and we stand on the  shoulders  of so many giants who came before us i  saw  his ability and capacity to behold  the weight of the struggle but also  there was a levity  to how he showed up and i think that's  incredibly powerful  for me um as a as an organizer who's  been in this struggle  and also for other younger organizers  younger than us  who are newly joining this fight what do  you think  congressman lewis is saying right now  continue to get in good trouble  continue to get in the way continue to  fight for justice and  freedom and equity and do it in a  non-violent way that  love is the foundation  we also talked about this moment right  here which we just heard speaker pelosi  mentioned and  what it meant to those co-founders of  black lives matter it's that image  of congressman lewis standing strong  there on black lives matter plaza you  know the co-founder  one of the co-founders of black lives  matter patrice cullers put it to me like  this she said what comes to mind when  she saw that photo  is that we are our ancestors wildest  dreams and she even said that what made  it special is that  congressman lewis was that ancestor but  he got to see his dream  to fruition before his death yeah that  was a very powerful moment eureka duncan  thank you
 

